Follow up Questions:
Can PUT create a resource?
Yes. If the client knows the resource URI and the server permits creation at that URI, 
PUT can create it. A successful creation normally returns 201 Created.

Is POST always non-idempotent?
POST is not inherently idempotent according to HTTP semantics, but an API can implement deduplication or 
idempotency keys to make retries safe for a particular operation.

Can we use POST to update a resource?
Yes. POST means process the submitted representation according to the target resource’s semantics, 
so it can update or trigger actions. However, use PUT or PATCH when their standardized replacement or 
partial-update semantics match the intended operation.

Is PUT safe?
No. Safe means the request is essentially read-only with respect to the requested resource operation. 
PUT changes resource state, although it is idempotent.

Which HTTP status codes should I return?
POST creation: commonly 201 Created with Location. Asynchronous processing: often 202 Accepted. 
PUT creation: 201 Created. Successful PUT update: 200 OK with a representation or 204 No Content without one.

What if a field is missing in a PUT request?
The API should follow its replacement contract. Do not silently treat missing fields as unchanged unless your 
API explicitly defines that behavior. Use PATCH for partial modification when appropriate.

Can PUT and POST both use the same URI?
Yes. For example, POST /employees can create a new employee in a collection, while PUT /employees/101 targets a 
specific employee. HTTP method semantics distinguish their operations.



